for f,suffix in [(source,'target'),(master,'reference')]:
    Image.open(f).convert('RGBA').crop(crop).resize((768,768),Image.Resampling.LANCZOS).save(p/f'{name}-{suffix}.png')
prompt='''Edit image1 ONLY: James West carrying walk phase0, fixed96x96native crop enlarged8x. Image2 is ACTUAL standing carrying master at identical native camera/scale and framing. Match image2 physical case width,height, panel proportions, dull silver four corner latches and distance from waist. Image1 case has roughly similar width but sits higher than image2, with different latch/grip proportions. Do not arbitrarily shrink the case; judge true panel edges separately from gloves. Rebuild case and coherent two-hand grip at image2 same low waist height and closer-in reach. Do not use any earlier walking draft as geometry authority. Keep one rigid rectangular graphite case, modest bevel/depth, original master silver latch scale, neutral matte finish. Case centered in front of waist toward LEFT; far glove wraps far LEFT edge; near glove wraps/supports RIGHT case edge, fingertips visible, wrist naturally connects existing near silver cuff. Bring both forearms into coherent carrying posture, elbows/body still actual image1 original pose. No detached hanging glove, stretched fingers, missing far hand, hand cutting through box, black hole behind case, enlarged cuff, cartoon outlines, chrome shine or purple tint. Preserve image1 head/neck/shoulder/torso above wrist region, belt/hip cloth and legs; never copy image2 thighs/legs or its equipment pixels. Image2's yellow meter reference is NOT an instruction to expose or duplicate tool. Keep image1 corrected belt tool occlusion: NO new near-side silver ring, wrench, meter/screen/buttons or yellow fleck. Actual long anatomical-right wrench is far side/body-occluded in this West view; original anatomical-left meter is higher belt behind carrying hand. Return full square image1 crop with ONLY authentic master-matched case/connected grips/necessary forearm correction. Preserve framing and transparent alpha. No scale fitting/regrading of body.'''
